## Core rules

- This is a consultation-workflow tool for doctors, not a generic portal or e-commerce system
- Use mock data and fake backend behavior freely where real backend is not ready
- Do not rewrite existing working screens unless explicitly asked
- Work one file or one small focused module at a time
- Before coding, continue from `docs/context/session_handoff.md` unless the user overrides it
- After coding, update `docs/context/session_handoff.md` before the final report
- When a design/product decision is agreed with Apurva, record it in `docs/context/decision_log.md` (decision, why,
  rejected options). When one changes, edit its entry — don't leave the old guidance standing
- Do not invent product logic if it is unclear
- If logic is missing, use a clearly marked `[MOCK ASSUMPTION]` and add it to `docs/context/open_questions.md`
- Respect the locked consultation flow — do not add CTAs or steps that are not part of the locked workflow
- **[UPDATED 2026-10-16, D-56]** The prototype is being **rebuilt from scratch**. The old `index.html` / `styles.css` / `app.js` are frozen as reference only (the live Netlify demo still serves them) — do not extend or fix them. The new build's file layout is set by the rebuild checklist; it uses only central design-system components and takes all text from `ui-screens/feedback/messages.json`. (Replaces the 2026-07-13 three-file rule.)

---

## What requires explicit user confirmation before implementing

Do not silently decide or implement the following — ask first:

- **Valid call threshold** — the 50-second minimum is locked, but exact timer behavior (pause, resume, reconnect) is open
- **Case type routing logic** — which fields determine Cat4 vs Pilot, and where these come from
- **HA skip eligibility** — what field or condition marks an order as HA-skipped
- **Transfer vs Forward distinction** — what exact operational difference these have downstream
- **No-pickup handling** — how many attempts before hold, what hold duration means
- **Calling model** — click-to-call now, in-app (VoIP) later; doctor-didn't-answer detection (OQ-027, D-56)
- **Doctor assignment rules** — how a case arrives in a doctor's queue and how re-assignment works (partly answered 2026-10-14: Back before the first attempted call releases the case — D-48; backend OQ-024/025)
- ~~**Medicine review authority**~~ — answered 2026-10-07: no add; prescribe or disable only; quantity is the backend's (`decision_log.md` D-04, D-06)
- **Notes behavior** — are notes visible to other teams? Are they locked after call completion?

If one of these surfaces during build and no clear answer exists, mark it `[OPEN DECISION]`, use a safe placeholder, and add it to `docs/context/open_questions.md`.

---

## Prototype optimization principle

This prototype optimizes for **visible demo speed** of the consultation workflow.

That means:
- build the doctor screen fast, mobile-first, with real-looking mock data
- use `[MOCK ASSUMPTION]` freely for non-critical gaps
- prefer a working believable demo state over theoretically complete but invisible UI
- a clean, modular rebuild (D-56) — not more patches on the old prototype

That does **not** mean:
- invent case-type routing logic
- fake the valid-call gate carelessly
- override `project_truth.md` for convenience

**Never silently invent** (moved from project_truth v1.0 §9): what makes an order Cat4 vs Pilot · Value vs Non-Value Meds · what sets HA required · HA-skip eligibility · doctor assignment source · notes visibility and locking. If one surfaces during build, mark `[OPEN DECISION]` and add it to `open_questions.md`.

---

## Decision labeling rule

Use these labels consistently:

- `[LOCKED]` — already decided, must be followed exactly
- `[RECOMMENDED]` — best current suggestion, open to override
- `[MOCK ASSUMPTION]` — temporary build unblocker, not final truth
- `[OPEN DECISION]` — requires user confirmation before implementing

---

## Confluence

Markdown in `docs/` is the source of truth; the Confluence page ([PROD space, "Truemeds Doctor Portal — PRD"](https://truemeds.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PROD/pages/1891303437/Truemeds+Doctor+Portal+PRD), top-level, no parent) is generated from it for stakeholder review/commenting. Never sync to Confluence unless explicitly told "sync" — edits made directly on Confluence during a review round are not automatically pulled back into this repo.

---

## Reporting rule after every coding task

After each coding task, Claude must output a short structured report:

1. **Files changed** — list every file that was modified or created
2. **What changed** — plain English summary
3. **What was intentionally not changed** — what was left alone and why
4. **Assumptions made** — any `[MOCK ASSUMPTION]` used, clearly labeled
5. **Still mocked / placeholder** — what is fake and needs real data or logic later
6. **Manual test plan** — 3 short steps: what to do, what to expect, any edge case to check

This report must be short and easy for a non-coder to read.

Before this report, update `docs/context/session_handoff.md` with the current status, files changed, validation done, known gaps, and next exact step.

---

## Final principle

Build like a real product shell, not a static wireframe.
Preserve the locked consultation workflow and locked valid-call gate.
Move fast where it is safe to move fast.
Stop and ask where the decision belongs to the user.

## Layout rule (do not change without Apurva)

Desktop = centred phone frame at the real phone shape 390 : 844 (D-97; was 9:16), controls in one column on the right, top-aligned, one scroll area, sheets/Rx/toasts inside the frame; mobile = full screen. Full rule: `design-system/RULES.md` §7. Run `python3 docs/layout_check.py` before committing any layout/CSS change. Never turn bottom sheets into desktop dialogs and never make the demo panel a scroll container.

## Logo (do not change without Apurva)
The header logo is the approved "truemeds for doctors" lockup in `brand/truemeds-for-doctors.svg` (project level, not in the design system). It embeds the canonical logo unchanged. Never redraw or recolour it. `python3 brand/check_lockup.py` must pass before any commit that touches `brand/`.

## Netlify deploys are on demand
Merges to `main` don't deploy the doctor-portal site (each production deploy costs Netlify credits). To deploy, put `[deploy]` in the pull request title; see the root `netlify.toml`. Only do this when Apurva asks for a deploy.
# project_truth.md — Truemeds Doctor Portal

**Status:** Approved by Apurva, 2026-10-16 (v2.0 — rewritten; replaces v1.0 of 2026-06-09, kept in git history)
**Changes:** Apurva only; every change is recorded in `docs/context/decision_log.md`. Detail lives in `ui-screens/` and `ui-screens/app-logic.md`.

---

## 1. What we're building

A mobile app for Truemeds doctors to take a case, call the patient, and confirm the prescription for that order — from sign-in to the next case.

Out of scope: admin, pharmacy operations, ordering, the customer app.

**Later (not in this version):** earnings (Wallet in Profile), pushed cases, calling inside the app.

## 2. Who uses it

Freelance doctors working with Truemeds, each on their own phone.

- They choose when to work: they set how long they're available and request cases one at a time. Today cases are not pushed to them; pushing cases may be explored later.
- How many cases a doctor handles depends on how long they're available. Today roughly 12,000 orders are handled by about 300 active doctors `[APPROX — Apurva to confirm both numbers and the period]`.
- They can see how many prescriptions they've confirmed today, on Home.
- In each case the doctor calls the patient, reviews every medicine on the order, and either confirms the prescription or ends the case another way (callback, patient unavailable).
- A good session: sign in fast, request a case, get through it without confusion, move straight to the next one.

## 3. The doctor's day

1. **Sign in** with mobile number and OTP. Fingerprint / Face ID can be turned on for faster sign-in.
2. **Home:** set availability (minimum 15 minutes), then request a case. Home shows the time left and prescriptions confirmed today.
3. **Open the case:** patient and order details, the prescription if attached, the medicines.
4. **Call the patient** from the app.
5. **Review each medicine:** prescribe it or disable it.
6. **Finish the case**, in one of these ways:
   - **Confirm prescription:** Cat4 cases, and Pilot cases where HA is skipped.
   - **Confirm & transfer:** Pilot, Value Meds, HA required — **version A, later.** The prescription is confirmed and the patient is handed to an HA on the same call. If the transfer fails, the case is forwarded instead. Launch (Fallback B, D-83 CW-F1): a Pilot live call shows **Confirm prescription**, then the HA check (B2 → transferring or forwarded).
   - **Confirm & forward:** Pilot, HA required, when the call ended before Confirm or there was no result (D-83 CW-F3, D-89). The prescription is confirmed on the partner's result and an HA calls the patient back separately.
   - **Skip HA:** the doctor decides HA isn't needed; the button then becomes Confirm prescription.
   - **Callback** or **Patient unavailable:** the case ends without a prescription.
7. **Next:** request the next case, or go back to Home.

## 4. Current rules

These rules apply until Apurva changes them. Every change is recorded in the decision log.

- **50-second call gate:** the final buttons appear only after the backend confirms a valid call — total talk time on the case of at least 50 seconds (a setting), adding up across calls on the same case. An **attempted call** = the patient answered (D-58).
- **Final button by case type:**

| Case type | HA status | Final button |
|---|---|---|
| Cat4 | — | Confirm prescription |
| Pilot | HA skipped (by customer, system or doctor) | Confirm prescription |
| Pilot | Value Meds + HA required | Confirm & transfer (version A, later; launch: Confirm prescription on the live call, then HA check) |
| Pilot | Non-Value Meds + HA required | Confirm & forward (only after the call has ended, no result or 16 / 7d). Launch: a live call shows Confirm prescription (B1) and then the HA check, the same as Value Meds |

  Skip HA appears only for Pilot cases where HA is required and not yet skipped. Cat4 cases never show anything HA-related. Each of the four rows is a separate outcome in analytics.
- **No unreviewed medicines:** Confirm is blocked while any medicine is still Pending.
- **Leaving a case:** header Back (on 1, 3 and 4 while the patient has never been reached) offers "Release case"; Back is hidden from the Call tap and never returns once the patient answered; once a call is attempted, the case can only end through the flow (confirm, callback, patient unavailable) (D-80 pt 1, D-58).
- **The doctor can't add medicines or change quantity.** Quantity is shown on the case, read-only.

## 5. Calling

Click-to-call through one telecom partner per flow: **Knowlarity** for Cat4 (one result after the call ends) and **Ozonetel** for Pilot transfer calls (live events). The doctor taps Call patient; the doctor's phone rings first, then the patient's; the doctor ends the call on the phone (no End call button). The app learns the call status by **polling** the backend (working assumption until Engineering confirms, OQ-030). If no call result arrives, the doctor is asked "Are you still on the call?" and can still confirm; the case then waits for the call record. A record under the gate, or none within the wait time, means the prescription is not confirmed → hold and retry; nothing goes to Ops (D-58, D-83 CW-D3/D4). Full flow: `ui-screens/app-logic.md` §4–§6.

## 6. Where the detail lives

- Screens: `ui-screens/` (final pictures of every state)
- Rules per screen: `ui-screens/app-logic.md`
- Why things are the way they are: `docs/context/decision_log.md`
- Still undecided: `docs/context/open_questions.md`
