8. **SHOULD-FIX · Available rows — long copy and widest timer —** §9.6 requires **“long content”** coverage. **Fix:** test the chosen prescription label, wrapped notices and `10:00:00` beside the standard Add button at 360. Allow labels to wrap; never shrink text, timer or targets to fit.

9. **BLOCKER · Chips + wheel — accessible interaction contract —** §9.8 requires visible focus and accessible controls. **Fix:** use one shared duration value; focus order follows chips→Hours→Minutes→Set. Chips expose their duration and `aria-pressed`; wheel columns expose names, current values and valid limits through a consistent spinbutton pattern. Keep focus on the tapped chip and announce once, for example, “Availability duration, 30 minutes.” Wheel changes update matching chip selection, including clearing it for custom values. Hide decorative wheel rows from duplicate reading and support keyboard/assistive gestures. [WAI spinbutton pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/spinbutton/)

10. **BLOCKER · Countdown — screen-reader and timing behaviour —** §9.8 says **“status changes announced politely; never steal focus.”** **Fix:** make remaining time readable on demand with spoken units; do not announce each tick. Announce near expiry and expiry once, and successful additions once. Document how extension and the cap meet Timing Adjustable; merely showing a countdown does not establish compliance. [WCAG 2.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/), [Timing Adjustable](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/timing-adjustable.html)

11. **SHOULD-FIX · Available action bar — Requesting / no cases / end confirmation —** §9.6 requires busy, empty and outcome coverage. **Fix:** include Requesting…, approved neutral “No cases right now” notice, and the End availability confirmation sheet with quiet Keep available. Preserve the existing request rules.

## Better

1. **Setup title + range:** make this the single shared label/helper for both controls. Keep Quick picks and Custom subordinate; do not add another selected-duration summary.

2. **Setup wheel:** retain the approved continuous selection band, large selected values, momentum and snap behaviour. State that a submit during movement uses the value currently in the band. A duration wheel is appropriate; Apple explicitly provides a countdown-duration picker mode. [Apple documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/uidatepicker/mode/countdowntimer)

3. **Available content card:** reuse the View Rx row’s trailing-button mechanics, not its entire anatomy. Remove decorative tiles and the prescription row’s empty action slot so text has room at 360.

4. **Time row:** reserve enough space for the widest timer and action. Keep the timer’s left edge and baseline stable when Add time appears, disappears or becomes disabled.

5. **Both screens’ specification:** add a compact state table naming each changed block, enabled controls, notice location and approved copy key. This makes “bottom bar unchanged” verifiable.

## Answers Q1–Q3

**Q1 — Section title + range.** Use **“Availability duration” / “15 minutes to 10 hours.”** The range governs chips and wheel together. Putting it in Custom’s brackets implies it applies only there. Accept scrolling with proper clearance rather than compressing targets.

**Q2 — “Prescriptions confirmed today.”** It describes the actual counting event in app-logic §3 and matches Confirm prescription. “Written” is understandable but less exact; “generated” and “created” could describe system activity. Treat this as proposed copy pending approval.

**Q3 — Keep Add time near expiry for this round.** That is approved behaviour; reserving its position solves movement without changing availability rules. Always-visible extension could improve convenience, but introduces early-addition and cap behaviour that is not yet specified. The five-minute threshold remains the documented mock value, not an external UX standard.
